README.md:7:CLI tools built for humans produce verbose, formatted output with ANSI colors, alignment padding, and all fields included. When AI agents consume this output, they pay 1.5-5x more tokens than necessary. MCP servers fix the format but add ~2,200 tokens of session overhead (tool definitions) and don't support batching.
README.md:21:### Why Not MCP?
README.md:24:- **No batching:** each query is a separate tool call (~80 tok framing each)
README.md:26:- **Identical output:** MCP and DSL return the same JSON when backed by the same field selection engine
README.md:166:| [Field Name Aliases in Schema-Once Output: Do They Save Tokens?](articles/field-alias-compression-study.md) | Three-part empirical study showing that field name abbreviations are architecturally redundant when compact tabular (schema-once) format already eliminates key repetition. Aliases save a fixed 5 tokens regardless of payload size, while schema discovery costs 85 tokens per roundtrip: a net loss in 75% of scenarios. |
README.md:177:| `references/comparison-example.md` | Real-world token measurement: MCP vs DSL vs Grep on a 346-element task board |
references/comparison-example.md:1:# Context Efficiency Comparison: MCP vs Mini-Query DSL vs Board Grep
references/comparison-example.md:20:5. **MCP and DSL produce identical JSON** for the same field selections -- both use the shared `internal/fields` package. MCP output measurements are therefore equal to DSL output measurements.
references/comparison-example.md:21:6. **Input overhead** measured as byte length of the command string (Bash) or JSON input body (MCP).
references/comparison-example.md:22:7. **Per-call framing** estimated at ~80 tokens per tool round-trip (50 tokens request frame + 30 tokens result frame). This is identical for Bash and MCP tool calls.
references/comparison-example.md:26:- **MCP**: 12 tool definitions loaded into the agent's context at session start. This is a one-time cost (~2,200 tokens) that persists for the entire session.
references/comparison-example.md:36:| Workflow | CLI (text) | DSL (JSON) | MCP (JSON) | Grep (text) |
references/comparison-example.md:49:| Workflow | CLI | DSL | MCP | Grep |
references/comparison-example.md:59:| Workflow | CLI | DSL | MCP | Grep |
references/comparison-example.md:70:- MCP counts include 3 separate MCP calls for W5 (3x framing overhead).
references/comparison-example.md:79:### MCP: one-time tool definition cost
references/comparison-example.md:92:If we include DSL syntax documentation in the system prompt (the `--help` text is ~500 bytes), that's ~125 tokens -- about 18x smaller than MCP tool definitions.
references/comparison-example.md:106:The question: **At how many queries does MCP's session overhead (2,200 tokens) pay for itself vs DSL?**
references/comparison-example.md:108:### Per-query savings: MCP vs DSL
references/comparison-example.md:110:MCP and DSL produce identical JSON output. The only per-query differences are:
references/comparison-example.md:112:| Factor | MCP | DSL | Delta |
references/comparison-example.md:114:| Input size | Slightly smaller (JSON vs command string) | Slightly larger | ~5-10 tokens MCP advantage |
references/comparison-example.md:117:| Batch capability | No (one call per operation) | Yes (semicolons in single call) | DSL advantage for batches |
references/comparison-example.md:119:**Single-query delta:** MCP saves ~5-10 tokens per query on input.
references/comparison-example.md:123:For batch queries (W5-style), the math reverses:
references/comparison-example.md:124:- DSL batch of 3: 144 tokens (1 call)
references/comparison-example.md:125:- MCP batch of 3: 338 tokens (3 calls)
references/comparison-example.md:126:- **DSL saves ~194 tokens per 3-element batch**
references/comparison-example.md:128:With batching factored in, MCP **never breaks even** against DSL for typical agent sessions.
references/comparison-example.md:130:### MCP vs CLI
references/comparison-example.md:132:CLI text output is sometimes smaller (W1: 179 vs 366 tokens) and sometimes larger (W5: 543 vs 34 tokens). The key advantage of structured approaches (MCP/DSL) is **field selection** -- the agent can request only what it needs.
references/comparison-example.md:137:- Break-even for MCP vs CLI: 2,200 / 144 = **~15 queries per session**
references/comparison-example.md:139:### MCP vs Grep
references/comparison-example.md:151:| Criterion | DSL | MCP | CLI | Grep |
references/comparison-example.md:163:- Any read operation: orientation, lookup, filtering, monitoring, batch queries
references/comparison-example.md:164:- Particularly dominant for batch operations (single call vs N calls)
references/comparison-example.md:167:**MCP (specific niche: long-lived sessions with external tool orchestration):**
references/comparison-example.md:168:- When the agent platform natively supports MCP and tool switching overhead matters more than context size
references/comparison-example.md:170:- When other MCP servers are already in use (marginal cost of adding one more is lower)
references/comparison-example.md:186:The Mini-Query DSL is the clear winner for agent workflows: it has zero session overhead (vs MCP's 2,200 tokens), supports batch queries in a single tool call (saving ~194 tokens per 3-element batch vs MCP's 3 separate calls), and produces the same structured JSON output as MCP. Board Grep is a niche tool for unstructured text search that fails catastrophically on structured queries (11K tokens for agent monitoring vs DSL's 686). MCP only becomes competitive in sessions exceeding ~300 queries, a threshold that typical agent tasks never reach.
references/comparison-example.md:197:| JSON (DSL/MCP output) | 3.0 | More punctuation characters, shorter key names |
